[{:adr/consequences "{:test_vector_integrity \"every hex literal in test/sha256d/core_test.cljc and the midstate fixture is copy-pasted from a freshly-run `python3 -c \\\"...hashlib...\\\"` / `shasum -a 256` invocation in this session's transcript, with explicit len()==64 assertions before use, not retyped from memory -- prompted by a live transcription near-miss (a hand-typed copy of the well-known million-'a' vector was one character short) during authoring\", :portability_bug_found_and_fixed \"sha256d.core/pad's length-field encoding originally shifted by up to 56 bits, valid on a JVM long (unsigned-bit-shift-right is 64-bit-aware there) but silently wrong under cljs (JS bitwise ops mask shift counts to 5 bits); the cljs_verify.cljs run under node caught it (2 of 4 known-answer checks + the midstate property check failed) where the JVM suite alone did not, since it never exercised the cljs code path. Fixed via sha256d.core/u64be-bytes (quot/mod split into 32-bit halves, no shift past bit 31); all 14 JVM tests / 5144 assertions and all 5 cljs checks pass after the fix.\", :honest_first_run_findings \"docs/evolution-log.md's initial entry records 3 clojure -M:evolve runs: no stable champion (2/3 runs land in a single 1%-proximity cluster), and evolve-round's recombination-only-among-elite-values causes generation-over-generation gene diversity loss -- both logged as real findings/follow-ups, not smoothed into a false 'discovery' narrative\", :non_duplication_of_adr_2607012230 \"cross-referenced the concurrently-landed kotoba-lang Bitcoin mining/wallet substrate ADR; the two are complementary (host-injected production crypto there vs from-scratch searchable/benchmarkable algorithm-level code here), not overlapping, and sha256d.core is noted as a candidate future host-crypto-free backend option for kotoba-lang/crypto (not attempted in this ADR)\"}", :adr/authoritative_for ["sha256d-clj lives at orgs/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj (github.com/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj, private), not under kotoba-lang -- cross-cutting -clj foundation lib per manifest/repos.edn's org-routing rule, not a language-substrate lib" "the matrixflow.net/case-study/110 reference is about AlphaProof/Gemini Deep Think/AlphaEvolve/FunSearch, NOT SHA-256/Bitcoin -- the transferable idea is the evolutionary-search-over-a-scored-population shape, reframed here with correctness as a hard gate (not a fitness term) because a hash function cannot be 'approximately' correct" "sha256d.core's round-function primitives (Ch/Maj/Sigma0/Sigma1/rotr32/add32) are shared .cljc with NO #?(:clj :cljs) split -- every op is bit-position-local or a bounded-width add, sign-representation-invariant on both platforms; the one place that genuinely needs a platform split is nothing in this repo post-fix, since the u64be-bytes fix made even the length-field encoding fully portable via quot/mod instead of wide shifts" "this repo is NOT a duplicate of ADR-2607012230's kotoba-lang/btc-crypto+btc-mining: that stack's SHA-256 is host-injected (kotoba-lang/crypto contract, vetted platform crypto for production use); sha256d-clj is deliberately from-scratch and host-crypto-free specifically so its formula/implementation-strategy space is searchable/benchmarkable, which a host crypto API black box structurally cannot be" "every test-vector hex literal in this repo was generated via Python hashlib / the system shasum utility and cross-checked by exact string length, never hand-transcribed from memory -- a live near-miss during authoring (a by-hand copy of the well-known million-'a' NIST vector came out one hex character short) is the concrete reason for this rule" "the cljs portability proof (test/sha256d/cljs_verify.cljs, run via `clojure -M:cljs && node target/cljs-verify.js`) caught a real bug the JVM test suite alone did not: JS bitwise shift operators mask the shift count to 5 bits (x >>> 40 behaves as x >>> 8), which broke the original pad function's 64-bit big-endian length-field encoding (fixed in sha256d.core/u64be-bytes by splitting into two 32-bit halves via quot/mod)" "docs/evolution-log.md's first entry is an honest negative result from an actual run, not a fabricated discovery: no stable champion across 3 runs (2 of 3 land in one proximity cluster -- Ranking found no significant difference at the current gene-pool size/noise floor), and evolve-round loses gene diversity generation-over-generation because recombination only reintroduces variants already present among elites" "ADDENDUM 2026-07-02: the tournament ran 17 rounds (see docs/evolution-log.md summary table). The ONLY lever that moved single-hash throughput was removing per-operation runtime overhead: compress-primitive-inline (JVM, unboxed round loop + inlined ch/maj) ~2.7x, compress-v8-inline (cljs, Int32Array) ~3.6x -- each a platform-only opt-in fast path, portable compress stays the default. Ch/Maj formula and schedule data structure NEVER mattered (measured, incl. a zero-alloc long-array schedule that only ties precompute); software 2-way multi-buffer is ~6% SLOWER (register spills). Allocation never mattered single-thread or parallel." "ADDENDUM 2026-07-02: the parallel nonce-search plateau (~4.6x on 10 cores) is all-core turbo FREQUENCY scaling, NOT GC/allocation -- proven by a GC-MXBean diagnostic (<1% pause) + an all-cores-hot test (single-thread drops to 46% = the per-thread parallel rate; 46% x 10 = 4.6). No code change lifts it; search-nonce-parallel scales as well as the hardware allows." "ADDENDUM 2026-07-02: mining composes midstate + fast compress via header-hash-with/search-nonce/search-nonce-parallel = ~4.4x per-nonce over naive full-header hashing (1.59x midstate x 2.78x fast compress), ~272k nonce/s on 10 cores, all bit-identical. Validated against the REAL Bitcoin genesis block (block 0, hash 000000000019d668...) on JVM+V8; search-nonce recovers Satoshi's genesis nonce 2083236893. Fixtures independently verified via Python hashlib." "ADDENDUM 2026-07-02 (cryptanalysis / inverse direction, sha256d.mitm + docs/preimage-mitm-cosci.md): below-brute-force PREIMAGE algorithms are real (MITM/splice-and-cut/biclique) and the co-scientist search DESIGNS them over the message-schedule dependency graph. run-mitm RUNS one on the real 32-bit round function: 8-round SHA-256 partial preimage in 2049 chunk-evals vs 2,325,617 brute = measured/verified ~1135x. Word-granularity dies at ~24 rounds; bit-level neutral bits give NO sound gain over word-level (tested: a bit of W_i is neutral for a chunk iff the whole word is unused). The ~45-round/2^255.5 record needs bicliques (differential trails) -- described, NOT fabricated." "ADDENDUM 2026-07-02: FULL 64-round SHA-256 is NOT broken and this repo does NOT fabricate a break. Honest reasoning recorded: a below-2^256 full-round preimage is conjectured not to exist; a co-scientist / AlphaEvolve / AlphaProof-Nexus-style search cannot conjure one (it searches within known-technique templates and a proof of a false statement cannot be found), and one-wayness is a circuit lower bound beyond known mathematics no prover can establish. Defensive risk: full-round break is a negligible tail (best 2^255.5 on 45/64 rounds; Grover only 2^128, sequential/infeasible); real risks are implementation/misuse (length-extension->HMAC, low-entropy->KDF, non-constant-time); AI-assisted cryptanalysis is a monitoring signal that may accelerate reduced-round records but not the full-round tail."], :adr/supersedes [], :adr/superseded_by [], :adr/topic "sha256d-clj", :adr/related ["CLAUDE.md" "manifest/repos.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607012230-kotoba-lang-btc-mining-wallet-substrate.edn" "orgs/kotoba-lang/eth-crypto/src/eth_crypto/core.cljc" "orgs/kotoba-lang/num/test/num/cljs_verify.cljs" "orgs/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj/README.edn" "orgs/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj/docs/evolution-log.md" "orgs/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj/docs/preimage-mitm-cosci.md" "orgs/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj/src/sha256d/mitm.cljc"], :adr/status "accepted", :adr/id "adr-2607012300-sha256d-clj-portable-cosci-tournament", :adr/doc_type "adr", :adr/last_verified "2026-07-02", :adr/body "# ADR-2607012300: sha256d-clj — portable .cljc SHA-256/SHA-256d + a Google-co-scientist/AlphaEvolve-shaped evolutionary benchmark tournament\n\n## Status\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nOwner request: design a repo, in `.cljc`, that efficiently derives Bitcoin's SHA-256,\ntaking a reference case study (`matrixflow.net/case-study/110`) and a \"Google\nco-scientist\" approach as inspiration.\n\nThe referenced case study turned out **not** to be about SHA-256 or Bitcoin at all --\n`WebFetch` on it showed it covers Google DeepMind's AlphaProof / Gemini Deep Think /\nAlphaEvolve / FunSearch line of work (LLM + evolutionary-search systems that discover\ngenuinely new algorithms; AlphaEvolve's headline result is a 48-multiplication\nalgorithm for 4x4 matrix multiplication, beating Strassen's 56-year-old bound). \"Google\nco-scientist\" is a second, related but distinct Google DeepMind system: a six-agent\narchitecture (Generation / Reflection / Ranking / Evolution / Proximity / Meta-review,\nunder a Supervisor) that runs a tournament-of-ideas over scientific hypotheses using\npairwise Elo ranking, drawn from AlphaGo's competitive-ranking lineage.\n\nBoth systems share a search shape (maintain a population of candidates, verify/score\neach, keep and recombine the best) that transfers to SHA-256 -- but with one\nconstraint neither AlphaEvolve's matrix-multiplication search nor co-scientist's\nhypothesis tournament has: **for a hash function, correctness is binary, not a\nscore.** A candidate that's 99% close to SHA-256 on some inputs isn't a slightly-worse\nSHA-256, it isn't SHA-256 at all. This reframing -- keep the search *shape*, make\ncorrectness a hard gate rather than a fitness term -- is this ADR's central design\ndecision, confirmed with the owner (`AskUserQuestion`) before implementation: a\nself-contained deterministic search (no LLM/Workflow calls needed at run time) over a\nhand-verified gene pool, executed as a real, immediately-runnable repo, rather than a\none-off `Workflow` invocation.\n\nPlacement: per `manifest/repos.edn`'s org-routing rule (\"cross-cutting 基盤 -clj →\ncom-junkawasaki\"), this is `orgs/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj`, not a kotoba-lang\nlanguage-substrate lib. `orgs/kotoba-lang/eth-crypto` was checked first for an existing\nraw SHA-256 implementation to avoid duplication -- it has none (its `hmac-sha256` is a\nprivate helper for RFC 6979 ECDSA nonce derivation, not a public from-scratch SHA-256).\n\n**Concurrent, related, and NOT overlapping in practice**: while this repo was being\nbuilt, ADR-2607012230 landed (`kotoba-lang/btc-crypto` + `kotoba-lang/btc-mining` +\n`kotoba-lang/mining-pool` + `kotoba-lang/wallet`), which also touches SHA-256d and\nBitcoin block-header mining. The two are complementary rather than duplicate: that\nstack's SHA-256 comes from `kotoba-lang/crypto`, a data contract with a **host-injected**\ncipher (a vetted platform crypto implementation, for production wallet/mining-pool\nuse) -- there is no algorithm-level surface to search or benchmark inside a host crypto\nAPI black box. This repo is deliberately the opposite: a from-scratch, host-crypto-free,\nalgorithm-level implementation, specifically so its internal formula/implementation-\nstrategy space is actually searchable and benchmarkable. If `kotoba-lang/crypto` ever\nwants a pure-`.cljc` backend option (no host dependency, e.g. for a WASM target without\na crypto API), `sha256d.core` here is a candidate to reuse; that integration is\nout of scope for this ADR.\n\n## Decision\n\nNew repo `orgs/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj` (GitHub, private, `com-junkawasaki` org):\n\n- **`sha256d.core`** -- FIPS 180-4 SHA-256 + `sha256d` (`sha256(sha256(x))`), over plain\n  sequences of byte values (ints 0-255), no host byte-array type in the hot path.\n  `compress`/`sha256-bytes`/`sha256d-bytes` take `ch-fn`/`maj-fn` as an explicit seam\n  (2-arity overloads default to this namespace's own `ch`/`maj`), so alternative\n  primitive formulations run through the exact same padding/schedule/compression\n  pipeline without duplicating it. This is the correctness oracle everything else in\n  the repo is checked against.\n- **`sha256d.ops`** -- the gene pool: `ch-alt` (`z ^ (x & (y^z))`, one fewer gate than\n  the FIPS textbook form) and `maj-alt` (`(x&y) | (z&(x|y))`), both real-world\n  formulations (OpenSSL/Bitcoin Core's C code uses them), each proven algebraically\n  equivalent to the reference primitive in a doc-comment and re-checked exhaustively\n  (all 8 single-bit truth-table rows + 2000 randomized 32-bit words per primitive) in\n  `test/sha256d/ops_test.cljc`.\n- **`sha256d.midstate`** -- Bitcoin block-header mining's classic optimization: an\n  80-byte header pads to exactly two 64-byte blocks; the first block (version +\n  prev-hash + a 36-byte merkle-root prefix) is constant while a miner iterates the\n  nonce for a fixed template, so caching the compression state after it (the\n  \"midstate\") skips 64 rounds of the *inner* SHA-256 per nonce attempt. Verified via\n  property test (500+200 random headers, `header-hash` via cached midstate == the\n  no-caching reference on every one) plus a fixture: a synthetic (explicitly\n  **not** the real genesis block, to avoid hand-transcribing a value from memory) 80-byte\n  header whose SHA-256d was computed independently via Python's `hashlib`, not\n  hand-derived.\n- **`sha256d.evolve`** -- the tournament: Generation (enumerate gene combinations) ->\n  Reflection (hard correctness gate: every candidate must match `sha256d.core`'s output\n  on 8 representative messages spanning the padding boundaries, or it's disqualified\n  outright, never merely down-scored) -> Ranking (pairwise benchmark, within-1%-is-a-draw,\n  Elo update starting at 1000, mirroring co-scientist's tournament-of-ideas) ->\n  Proximity (cluster ranked results within 1% so Meta-review reports ties honestly\n  instead of a spurious single winner) -> Evolution (recombine elite gene choices for\n  the next generation) -> Meta-review, under a `run-tournament` Supervisor.\n- Portability proof, not just claim: `test/sha256d/cljs_verify.cljs`, compiled via\n  `clojure -M:cljs` and run under `node` (mirroring `com-junkawasaki/num-clj`'s\n  `cljs-verify` pattern). This actually caught a real bug during development (see\n  Consequences) that the JVM suite alone did not.\n- Standard scaffold: `deps.edn` (zero third-party deps for the core; `cognitect\n  test-runner` for `:test`; `org.clojure/clojurescript` for `:cljs`), `README.edn`,\n  `.gitignore`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (JDK 17/21 matrix `clojure -M:test` +\n  a `cljs-portability` job running the node proof), `docs/evolution-log.md`\n  (append-only tournament findings).\n\n## Consequences\n\n- **All test vectors are independently computed, not hand-transcribed.** Every hex\n  literal in `test/sha256d/core_test.cljc` and the midstate fixture was generated with\n  Python's `hashlib`/the system `shasum` and cross-checked by exact string length\n  before use, after a live near-miss during authoring: a by-hand transcription of the\n  well-known \"1,000,000 x 'a'\" NIST vector was one hex character short, caught only by\n  comparing against a freshly-computed value rather than trusting memory a second time.\n  The lesson generalizes: never hand-type a cryptographic ground-truth constant into a\n  test; always regenerate it from an independent, trusted tool.\n- **The cljs proof caught a real portability bug the JVM suite could not.**\n  `sha256d.core/pad`'s original 64-bit big-endian length-field encoding shifted a\n  JVM `long` by up to 56 bits, which is valid there (`unsigned-bit-shift-right` on a\n  64-bit `long`) but silently wrong under ClojureScript: JS bitwise operators are\n  32-bit only and mask any shift count to its low 5 bits (`x >>> 40` behaves as\n  `x >>> 8`), corrupting the length field for any input needing shifts past bit 31.\n  Fixed by splitting the 64-bit value into two 32-bit halves via `quot`/`mod` (portable\n  arithmetic, not shifting past bit 31) in `sha256d.core/u64be-bytes`. All 14 JVM tests\n  (5144 assertions) and all 5 cljs checks pass after the fix. This is the concrete\n  reason `.cljc` claims of portability in this codebase should be proven by actually\n  compiling+running under cljs, not inferred from \"it's bitwise code, should be fine.\"\n- **The tournament's first real run is an honest negative result, not a discovery.**\n  `docs/evolution-log.md` records three `clojure -M:evolve` runs: no stable champion\n  (2 of 3 runs land in a single 1%-proximity cluster -- Ranking itself found no\n  significant difference), and a generation-over-generation diversity-loss pattern in\n  `evolve-round` (recombination only reintroduces gene values already present among\n  elites, so a gene collapses to one variant for the rest of a run once both elites\n  happen to agree on it -- itself mostly driven by the benchmark noise in the first\n  finding, not real selective pressure). Both are logged as explicit follow-ups rather\n  than smoothed over.\n- **West registration.** `manifest/repos.edn` `:extra-projects` gained one entry;\n  `manifest/west.yml` gained the matching one entry, hand-inserted rather than via a\n  full `nbb scripts/gen-west-manifest.cljs` regen, because `--check` was already STALE\n  before this change (pre-existing, unrelated child-repo pin drift from concurrent\n  activity) -- a full regen+commit would have bundled that unrelated drift into this\n  registration. The hand-inserted entry was verified byte-for-byte identical to what\n  the generator itself produces for this path (regenerated to a scratch copy, diffed,\n  reverted, matched) before being committed, and `west update sha256d-clj` resolves\n  and checks out the pinned revision (`847da43...`) cleanly. `origin/main` showed a\n  `(forced update)` warning + `unrelated histories` on fetch/merge during this session;\n  `gh api compare` confirmed it was the known shallow-clone false-positive (`status:\n  ahead, behind_by: 0, merge_base_commit == old local tip`), resolved via\n  `git fetch --deepen=20` per CLAUDE.md, not treated as a real force-push.\n\n## Alternatives\n\n- **A real `Workflow`-orchestrated multi-agent loop** (literal Generation/Reflection/\n  Ranking/Evolution/Proximity/Meta-review subagents proposing and verifying code diffs\n  each run, more faithful to AlphaEvolve/co-scientist) -- rejected for this pass per\n  owner's explicit choice (`AskUserQuestion`): it costs tokens on every run and needs\n  Workflow opt-in, versus a self-contained deterministic harness that ships in the repo\n  and needs no LLM to operate. Left as a documented possible follow-up, not implemented.\n- **Scoring correctness as a fitness term instead of a hard gate** (closer to how\n  AlphaEvolve scores an imperfect matrix-multiplication algorithm) -- rejected: SHA-256\n  has no meaningful notion of \"partial credit,\" so this would just be a more confusing\n  way to implement the same disqualify-on-mismatch behavior.\n- **Hand-deriving the real Bitcoin genesis block header as a test fixture** -- rejected\n  in favor of a clearly-labeled synthetic header cross-checked via Python `hashlib`,\n  specifically to avoid the same class of transcription risk the million-`a` vector\n  near-miss demonstrated, for a 160-hex-character value with no independent local\n  oracle to check it against at authoring time.\n\n## Follow-ups (explicitly out of scope here)\n\n- Grow `sha256d.ops`'s gene pool (loop-unrolling degree, message-schedule buffer reuse,\n  batch/lane-parallel hashing) -- today's search space (2 primitives x 2 variants) is\n  small enough that Ranking's findings are dominated by benchmark noise, per the\n  evolution-log's first entry.\n- Run `sha256d.evolve/run-tournament` under node, not just the JVM (the cljs\n  portability proof covers `core`/`ops`/`midstate` correctness, not the tournament\n  itself).\n- If desired, offer `sha256d.core` as an alternative host-crypto-free backend option for\n  `kotoba-lang/crypto`'s SHA-256 contract (see Context) -- not attempted here.\n\n## Addendum (2026-07-02): the full 17-round search, mining, real-genesis validation, and the cryptanalysis pivot\n\nThe initial scaffold above was iterated as a running co-scientist tournament (17 rounds, all in\n`orgs/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj/docs/evolution-log.md`, which has a summary table at its top).\nEvery result is bit-identical-gated and, where a speedup is claimed, measured. Net outcome:\n\n**Forward direction (efficient derivation).** The tournament *rejected* four plausible optimizations\nafter measuring them -- Ch/Maj round-primitive formula (noise on JVM & V8, boxed & unboxed),\nschedule data structure (`:rolling` ~7-10% slower, `:precompute-transient`/`:mutable` tie the\nreference: even a zero-allocation `long-array` schedule does not beat the persistent-vector\nprecompute), and software 2-way multi-buffer interleave (~6% slower: register spills > ILP gain).\nThe one lever that moved single-hash throughput was **removing per-operation runtime overhead**:\n`compress-primitive-inline` (JVM, unboxed round loop + inlined ch/maj) ~2.7x; `compress-v8-inline`\n(cljs, `Int32Array` + fixed-arity int32 arithmetic) ~3.6x -- each a JVM- resp. cljs-only opt-in\nfast path, with the portable `compress` staying the default. Portable *relative* verdicts were\nconfirmed to reproduce on V8 (round 8); the parallel nonce-search plateau (~4.6x on 10 cores) was\ndiagnosed (round 14) as all-core turbo frequency scaling, NOT GC/allocation (a cheap GC-MXBean +\nall-cores-hot diagnostic refuted the allocation hypothesis and correctly stopped an allocation-free\npath from being built). Allocation never mattered anywhere -- single-thread or parallel.\n\n**Mining.** `sha256d.midstate` composes with the fast path via `header-hash-with`/`search-nonce`/\n`search-nonce-parallel` for ~4.4x per-nonce over naive full-header hashing (1.59x midstate x 2.78x\nfast compress, stacking cleanly), ~272k nonce/s on 10 cores, all bit-identical.\n\n**Real-world grounding.** The whole stack (core sha256d + midstate + fast path) was validated\nagainst the **actual Bitcoin genesis block** (block 0, hash `000000000019d668...`) on JVM and V8,\nand `search-nonce` recovers Satoshi's real genesis nonce 2083236893 -- the first non-synthetic\nvalidation, with fixtures independently verified via Python hashlib.\n\n**Cryptanalysis pivot (inverse direction).** The owner then redirected from *forward* optimization to\nthe *inverse* problem: use the co-scientist approach to invert SHA-256 (find a preimage) below the\n2^256 brute-force bound. `sha256d.mitm` (see `docs/preimage-mitm-cosci.md`) delivers the honest\nanswer:\n- Below-brute-force preimage algorithms are real (meet-in-the-middle / splice-and-cut / biclique).\n  The co-scientist search *designs* them by finding neutral-word splits over the message-schedule\n  dependency graph, and `run-mitm` **runs one on the real 32-bit round function**: 8-round SHA-256,\n  a partial preimage found in 2,049 compression-chunk evals vs brute force's 2,325,617 -- a measured,\n  verified **~1135x** speedup (the MITM square root, on real rounds, not a toy cipher).\n- It *locates the wall*: word-granularity MITM dies at ~24 rounds when the message expansion's\n  dependency fan-out makes every base word feed both chunks. Bit-level neutral bits give NO gain over\n  word-level under sound analysis (tested): a bit of W_i is neutral for a chunk iff the whole word is\n  unused. The published ~45-round / 2^255.5 record needs bicliques (differential trails), which this\n  repo describes but does **not** fabricate a complexity for.\n- **Full 64-round SHA-256 stays unbroken by any meaningful margin, and this repo does not fabricate a\n  break.** The honest reasoning was recorded (including why an AlphaProof-Nexus-style formal-proof AI\n  cannot help either: a fast inversion is conjectured false so no proof can be searched for it, and\n  one-wayness is a circuit lower bound beyond known mathematics that no prover can establish -- SHA-256\n  sits in the deliberate gap between provably-true and provably-false).\n\n**Defensive risk framing** (discussed, not yet doc'd): full-round preimage break risk is a negligible\ntail (best attack 2^255.5 on 45/64 rounds; ~17 years of zero full-round margin; Grover only reaches\n2^128, sequential/infeasible); the real practical risks are implementation/misuse (length-extension ->\nHMAC, low-entropy -> KDF, non-constant-time compare); AI-assisted formal/automated cryptanalysis\n(AlphaProof/co-scientist style) is a monitoring signal that may *accelerate reduced-round records* but\ndoes not change the full-round tail risk. A `docs/sha256-preimage-risk.md` capturing this is an open\nfollow-up.\n\n**Shipped API:** `compress` (portable reference) · `compress-primitive-inline` (JVM ~2.7x) ·\n`compress-v8-inline` (V8 ~3.6x) · `midstate`/`header-hash`/`search-nonce`/`search-nonce-parallel` ·\n`mitm` (reduced-round preimage-attack search + runnable demo). Correctness gated throughout\n(clojure -M:test: 29 tests / 8000 assertions; cljs-verify 8/8), verified against NIST vectors and the\nreal Bitcoin genesis block.", :adr/decision "{:new_repo {:path \"orgs/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj\", :remote \"com-junkawasaki\", :visibility :private, :method \"clojure -M:test green (14 tests, 5144 assertions) + clojure -M:cljs && node target/cljs-verify.js green (5 checks) BEFORE git init/commit; git init, one commit, gh repo create com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj --private --source=. --remote=origin --push\", :head_sha \"847da432443ad401dd1d028578a980c4265bb05b\", :modules {:core \"FIPS 180-4 SHA-256 + sha256d (double SHA-256) over plain byte-value sequences; ch-fn/maj-fn injection seam via 2/4-arity compress and sha256-bytes/sha256d-bytes overloads\", :ops \"gene pool: ch-alt (z ^ (x & (y^z))), maj-alt ((x&y) | (z&(x|y))) -- both algebraically proven equivalent in doc-comments and exhaustively re-checked in test\", :midstate \"Bitcoin header mining midstate caching: cache compression state after the header's constant first 64-byte chunk, skip re-compressing it per nonce attempt\", :evolve \"Generation/Reflection/Ranking/Evolution/Proximity/Meta-review tournament under run-tournament; reflect is a hard correctness gate, rank does pairwise Elo benchmarking with a 1%-is-a-draw threshold\"}}, :manifest_registration {:repos_edn \"added \\\"orgs/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj\\\" to :extra-projects (local edit + commit; superproject was confirmed in sync with origin/main via git fetch + gh api compare + git merge --ff-only immediately beforehand, so no API-single-entry-commit workaround was needed for this file)\", :west_yml \"added one project entry (name/remote/revision/path/groups) by hand at the correct alphabetical position, NOT via a full nbb scripts/gen-west-manifest.cljs regen -- --check was already STALE before this change (pre-existing unrelated child-repo pin drift from concurrent activity); verified byte-for-byte identical to the generator's own output for this path by running a full regen to a scratch copy, diffing, and reverting before committing\", :verify \"pin (847da43...) == orgs/com-junkawasaki/sha256d-clj@main HEAD confirmed; `west update sha256d-clj` resolves and checks out the pinned revision cleanly (left a normal detached-HEAD-then-checkout-main note, not an error)\", :deliberately_not_done \"nbb scripts/gen-west-manifest.cljs was run to completion once to produce a scratch diff for verification, then its output was discarded (restored the hand-edited version) rather than committed, to avoid bundling ~dozens of unrelated pre-existing pin advances into this one-entry registration -- same pin-regression-trap avoidance as ADR-2607011816\"}}", :db/id -1, :adr/problem "{:source \"user request 2026-07-01 (JP): 'bitcoin の sha256 を https://www.matrixflow.net/case-study/110/ などを参考に効率的に、導出する repo を cljc で設計してみて, google cosientist の アプローチで' -- design a cljc repo that efficiently derives Bitcoin's SHA-256, referencing the matrixflow case study, using a 'Google co-scientist' approach\", :investigated [\"WebFetch on matrixflow.net/case-study/110 showed it covers AlphaProof/Gemini Deep Think/AlphaEvolve/FunSearch, not SHA-256 or Bitcoin at all -- corrected the premise to the user before designing\" \"WebSearch confirmed Google co-scientist's 6-agent architecture (Generation/Reflection/Ranking/Evolution/Proximity/Meta-review under a Supervisor, Elo-based tournament-of-ideas) and AlphaEvolve's evolutionary-program-database + LLM-mutation search shape (48-multiplication 4x4 matrix mult, beating Strassen's 56-year bound)\" \"grep across manifest/repos.edn org-routing comment confirmed 'cross-cutting 基盤 -clj -> com-junkawasaki' placement rule; grepped orgs/kotoba-lang/eth-crypto/src/eth_crypto/core.cljc and confirmed no existing from-scratch public SHA-256 to avoid duplicating (only a private hmac-sha256 helper for ECDSA nonce derivation)\" \"inspected orgs/kotoba-lang/num and orgs/kotoba-lang/eth-crypto as templates for deps.edn/README/CI-workflow/cljs-verify-pattern conventions\" \"AskUserQuestion with the owner resolved two open design forks before writing code: (1) self-contained deterministic search vs a real Workflow-orchestrated multi-agent loop -- chose self-contained/deterministic; (2) design-doc-only vs full scaffold+push now -- chose full pipeline now, per CLAUDE.md's standing new-project authorization\" \"mid-session, git fetch on the superproject showed '(forced update)' + 'refusing to merge unrelated histories' on origin/main -- gh api compare confirmed the documented shallow-clone false-positive pattern (status: ahead, behind_by: 0, merge_base_commit == old local tip), resolved via git fetch --deepen=20 rather than treated as a real force-push; the fast-forward pulled in a concurrent, unrelated ADR-2607012230 (kotoba-lang Bitcoin mining/wallet substrate) which was then cross-referenced for a non-duplication argument\"], :risk \"a hash function has no partial credit -- an evolutionary/co-scientist-style search that treats correctness as a soft fitness term (as AlphaEvolve does for matrix multiplication, where a slightly-wrong answer is just a lower score) would produce something that looks like it's exploring implementations while silently accepting incorrect ones; mitigated by making sha256d.evolve/reflect a hard disqualifying gate, run before any benchmarking, never contributing to the Elo score\"}", :adr/followups ["grow sha256d.ops's gene pool (loop-unrolling degree, message-schedule buffer reuse, batch/lane-parallel hashing) -- today's 2x2 search space is small enough that Ranking's findings are dominated by benchmark noise per the evolution-log's first entry" "run sha256d.evolve/run-tournament under node, not just the JVM -- the cljs portability proof covers core/ops/midstate correctness only, not the tournament itself" "evaluate offering sha256d.core as an alternative host-crypto-free backend for kotoba-lang/crypto's SHA-256 data contract -- not attempted in this ADR"], :adr/alternatives "[{:option \"a real Workflow-orchestrated multi-agent loop (literal Generation/Reflection/Ranking/Evolution/Proximity/Meta-review subagents proposing and verifying code diffs each run)\", :rejected_because \"owner's explicit choice via AskUserQuestion: costs tokens per run and needs Workflow opt-in, versus a self-contained deterministic harness that ships in the repo and needs no LLM to operate at run time; left as a documented possible follow-up\"} {:option \"score correctness as a fitness term instead of a hard gate, closer to how AlphaEvolve scores an imperfect matrix-multiplication algorithm\", :rejected_because \"SHA-256 has no notion of partial credit -- would just be a more confusing way to implement the same disqualify-on-mismatch behavior sha256d.evolve/reflect already does directly\"} {:option \"hand-derive the real Bitcoin genesis block header as the midstate test fixture, for a more famous/recognizable example\", :rejected_because \"same transcription-risk class as the million-'a' near-miss, for a 160-hex-character value with no independent local oracle to check it against at authoring time; used a clearly-labeled synthetic header cross-checked via Python hashlib instead\"}]", :adr/authoritative true, :adr/title "ADR-2607012300: sha256d-clj -- portable .cljc SHA-256/SHA-256d + a Google-co-scientist/AlphaEvolve-shaped evolutionary benchmark tournament", :adr/source-format "edn"}]